\tau_{rec}

 tends to infinity, meaning the oscillation would effectively not recur if it's not being driven or reinforced by the surface.

stellar-seismology-6e801f06.conceptual_definition:
Stellar seismology, also known as asteroseismology, is the study of oscillations within stars. These oscillations, like seismic waves on Earth, carry information about the star's internal structure and dynamics. The given formula, 

\tau_{rec} = \frac{I}{I_{sf}} 

represents the characteristic recurrence time (

\tau_{rec}

) of certain types of stellar oscillations or waves, particularly those associated with convective envelopes or other complex internal regions. It quantifies how often a specific wave packet or disturbance is expected to reappear or be reinforced within the star, influenced by its internal properties and the nature of the surface phenomena it interacts with.

guidance-equation-bohmian-motion-3c78162.interpretation:
The equation 

$$ 
\mathbf{v}_i = \frac{d\mathbf{Q}_i}{dt} = \frac{\hbar}{m_i} \text{Im} \left( \frac{\nabla_i \psi}{\psi} \right) 
$$ 

defines the velocity 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} \mathbf{v}_i \end{tabular}

 of the $i$-th particle at position 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} \mathbf{Q}_i \end{tabular}

. This velocity is the rate of change of the particle's position 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}  rac{d\mathbf{Q}_i}{dt} \end{tabular}

. The velocity is proportional to the imaginary part of the ratio of the gradient of the wave function 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} 
abla_i  oldsymbol{\psi} \end{tabular}

 to the wave function itself 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}  oldsymbol{\psi} \end{tabular}

. The constant 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}  oldsymbol{\hbar} \end{tabular}

 is the reduced Planck constant, and 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} m_i \end{tabular}

 is the mass of the particle. The term 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}  rac{ oldsymbol{\nabla}_i  oldsymbol{\psi}}{ oldsymbol{\psi}} \end{tabular}

, often referred to as the logarithmic derivative, carries information about the phase and amplitude gradients of the wave function. Specifically, if 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}  oldsymbol{\psi}(\mathbf{Q}, t) = R(\mathbf{Q}, t) e^{iS(\mathbf{Q}, t)/\boldsymbol{\hbar}} \end{tabular}

, where 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} R \end{tabular}

 is the amplitude and 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}} S \end{tabular}

 is the phase, then 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}  rac{ oldsymbol{\nabla}  oldsymbol{\psi}}{ oldsymbol{\psi}} =  rac{ oldsymbol{\nabla} R}{R} +  rac{i  oldsymbol{\nabla} S}{ oldsymbol{\hbar}} \end{tabular}

. The imaginary part of this expression is 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}  rac{ oldsymbol{\nabla} S}{ oldsymbol{\hbar}} \end{tabular}

. Therefore, the velocity becomes 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}  oldsymbol{v}_i =  rac{ oldsymbol{\nabla} S}{m_i} \end{tabular}

, indicating that the particle's velocity is proportional to the gradient of the phase of its wave function, which dictates the direction of motion. This is analogous to how the direction of propagation of a wave is determined by the wavefronts (surfaces of constant phase).

guidance-equation-bohmian-motion-3c78162.limits_and_boundary:
In the classical limit, where quantum effects become negligible (e.g., for macroscopic objects or large quantum numbers), the de Broglie wavelength approaches zero. In this context, the wave function's phase gradients change very slowly, and the guidance equation approaches Newton's second law of motion. The term 

 egin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}  rac{ oldsymbol{\nabla} S}{m_i} \end{tabular}

 can be related to the acceleration of the particle. As the wave function becomes more localized or its phase becomes effectively constant over the scale of the particle's motion, the velocity tends to follow classical trajectories. Conversely, in highly quantum regimes with rapidly varying wave function phases and complex interference patterns, the guidance equation shows the intricate and non-classical paths that particles can take.

